<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Fei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 221</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Fei was a general of the late Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> from the start of his career together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>. Famed for his courage and fierce temper, he held the bridge at Changban in 208 to cover Liu Bei's retreat. He was murdered by his own officers in 221 as Shu prepared to attack Wu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-fei.jpg" alt="Zhang Fei" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White whirlwind · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Fei_(Chinese_characters).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhang Fei, courtesy name Yide, came from Zhuo Commandery. In the 180s, near the end of the Eastern Han dynasty, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a> became followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>. Guan Yu was several years older, and Zhang regarded him as an elder brother. Both were known as stalwart and strong men and talented fighters, and they acted as Liu Bei's bodyguards. He cherished them as brothers, and the three of them shared a room, a mat and a pot. In the novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a>, written in the 14th century, they became sworn brothers in the fictional Oath of the Peach Garden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 194 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> succeeded Tao Qian as Governor of Xu Province and left Zhang Fei to guard Xiapi. Zhang Fei wanted to kill Cao Bao, a former officer of Tao Qian, for reasons unknown. Cao Bao appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a>, who seized Xiapi, and Zhang Fei fled. In 208 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>'s cavalry caught Liu Bei's followers at Changban. Liu Bei fled with only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>, Zhang Fei, Zhao Yun and a few soldiers, while Zhang Fei led 20 horsemen to cover the retreat. He destroyed a bridge, stood at one end with his spear and shouted: I'm Zhang Yide. You can come forth and fight me to the death! Cao Cao's soldiers did not dare approach, and Liu Bei escaped.</p><p>Around 212 Zhang Fei reinforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> in Yi Province, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yi">Zhang Yi</a> at Deyang and taking Jiangzhou, where he captured the officer Yan Yan. Yan Yan answered his anger with defiance, and Zhang Fei, impressed, released him and treated him as an honoured guest. Liu Zhang surrendered Yi Province in 214. In around 215 Zhang Fei defeated Zhang He in Baxi Commandery after the two armies had held their positions for over 50 days. In 219 he was made General of the Right, though Liu Bei chose Wei Yan to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 221 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> founded Shu Han and promoted Zhang Fei to General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariots</a> and Cavalry. Around July or August that year, when Liu Bei campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> to avenge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>, Zhang Fei was ordered to lead 10,000 troops from Langzhong to join him at Jiangzhou. He set two subordinates, Fan Qiang and Zhang Da, an apparently impossible task, and they, fearing punishment, killed him and defected to Wu with his head. The historian Chen Shou wrote that Zhang Fei was brutal and heartless towards his men, a flaw that led to his downfall, and Liu Bei had warned him against excessive punishments. Rafe de Crespigny calls him arrogant, impetuous and brutal, though courteous to the virtuous.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Fei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Warlords then fight for territory: Cao Cao defeats Yuan Shao at Guandu in the summer of 200, and Liu Bei, with his oath brothers Guan Yu and <b>Zhang Fei</b>, and the strategist Zhuge Liang join Sun Quan to defeat Cao Cao at the Battle of Red Cliffs.</blockquote><a href="/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The later novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms made Liu Bei, Guan Yu, <b>Zhang Fei</b> and Zhuge Liang lasting heroes.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhuozhou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Langzhong, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.49,115.99&amp;year=211" aria-label="Zhuozhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7415 749 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhang Fei"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7865 1019h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7865 1019h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.49,115.99&amp;year=211">What was here in 211 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
